// 51a — Req itself, reached with NO compiler change at all.
//
// The dependency tree is whatever `mix deps.get && mix deps.compile` produced.
// mix lays `_build/dev/lib/<app>/ebin` out one app per directory, which is
// exactly the shape ERL_LIBS already means, so the two paths below are the
// entire integration:
//
//   ERL_LIBS=/opt/homebrew/lib/elixir/lib:/tmp/reqprobe/_build/dev/lib \
//   bsc --src-root wayfinder/prototypes/51a-code-path \
//       wayfinder/prototypes/51a-code-path/Req Tag
//
// No HTTP is performed here. `Req.new/1` builds the request struct and returns
// it, which is all that is needed to answer ticket 50's question about what a
// foreign aggregate looks like on arrival.
// MEASURED 2026-08-21 — all four, and the compiler delta is ZERO.
//
//   Tag()      -> :'Elixir.Req.Request'   the struct arrives carrying its own tag
//   Method()   -> :get                     an ordinary field reads through :maps.get
//   HasKind()  -> :false                   no `Kind` key, so no record pattern can match it
//   Start()    -> (:ok, [:compiler, :elixir, :logger, :crypto, :asn1, :public_key,
//                        :ssl, :hpax, :mint, :nimble_pool, :nimble_options,
//                        :telemetry, :mime, :finch, :jason, :req])
//
// `bsc` needed no flag, no change and no tool. ERL_LIBS is honoured by the BEAM
// code server, an escript inherits it, and `mix deps.compile` already emits the
// layout it means. Ticket 51 candidate 1 turns out to be cheaper than written:
// not "one flag", but none.
//
// Two caveats the result does not remove. Elixir itself must be installed and on
// the path — `Start()` reports `:elixir` among the applications it brought up —
// so this is interop with a neighbour's runtime, not a self-contained artefact.
// And ERL_LIBS is an environment variable, so nothing in the language or the
// spec records which dependencies a program needs; that is the part of ticket 51
// a flag or a manifest would actually buy.
module Req

using :'Elixir.Req' {
    // Returns `%Req.Request{}` — a struct, so a map, so `term` is the honest
    // declaration until ticket 50 decides how a foreign aggregate is named.
    term new(list<(atom, term)> opts)
}

using :'Elixir.Application' {
    term ensure_all_started(atom app)
}

using :maps {
    term get(atom k, term m)
    bool is_key(atom k, term m)
}

// 1. Does the struct arrive, and what tag does it carry?
//    THIS IS TICKET 50'S QUESTION, ANSWERED BY A REAL LIBRARY.
public term Tag()
Tag() -> :maps.get(:'__struct__', Built())

// 2. An ordinary field off the same struct.
public term Method()
Method() -> :maps.get(:method, Built())

// 3. Does a beam-sharp record's key exist on it? It must not — a record mints
//    `Kind` from its own qualified name, and Req has never heard of that.
public bool HasKind()
HasKind() -> :maps.is_key(:'Kind', Built())

// 4. Starting the application. Req runs a Finch supervision tree, so this is
//    the half that a pure stdlib call never exercises.
public term Start()
Start() -> :'Elixir.Application'.ensure_all_started(:req)

private term Built()
Built() -> :'Elixir.Req'.new([(:url, "https://example.com"), (:method, :get)])
